Here's a simple Chrome extension that allows you to save the current webpage's content as an HTML file. Save these files in a folder and load it as an unpacked extension in Chrome:

### 1. Create `manifest.json` (Configuration)
```json
{
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "Save Page Content",
  "version": "1.0",
  "permissions": [
    "activeTab",
    "scripting"
  ],
  "action": {
    "default_popup": "popup.html",
    "default_icon": {
      "16": "icon16.png",
      "48": "icon48.png",
      "128": "icon128.png"
    }
  },
  "icons": {
    "16": "icon16.png",
    "48": "icon48.png",
    "128": "icon128.png"
  }
}
```

### 2. Create `popup.html` (User Interface)
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Save Page</title>
    <style>
        body {
            width: 200px;
            padding: 10px;
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
        }
        button {
            width: 100%;
            padding: 10px;
            font-size: 14px;
            cursor: pointer;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <h3>Save Current Page</h3>
    <button id="saveBtn">Save Page Content</button>
    <script src="popup.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

### 3. Create `popup.js` (Main Logic)
```javascript
document.getElementById('saveBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
    chrome.tabs.query({active: true, currentWindow: true}, (tabs) => {
        const tab = tabs[0];

        // Get the page content via script injection
        chrome.scripting.executeScript({
            target: {tabId: tab.id},
            func: () => document.documentElement.outerHTML
        }, (results) => {
            if (chrome.runtime.lastError) {
                alert('Could not save content: ' + chrome.runtime.lastError.message);
                return;
            }

            if (!results || !results[0] || !results[0].result) {
                alert('Failed to retrieve page content.');
                return;
            }

            // Create a downloadable file
            const content = results[0].result;
            const blob = new Blob([content], {type: 'text/html'});
            const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);

            // Create hidden link and trigger download
            const a = document.createElement('a');
            a.href = url;
            a.download = 'page_content_' + new Date().toISOString().replace(/:/g, '-') + '.html';
            document.body.appendChild(a);
            a.click();
            document.body.removeChild(a);
            URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
        });
    });
});
```

### 4. Add Icons (Required for Extension)
You'll need three icon files in your folder:
- `icon16.png` (16x16 pixels)
- `icon48.png` (48x48 pixels)
- `icon128.png` (128x128 pixels)

You can use simple placeholder icons like:
```text
icon16.png: A small square icon (e.g., a disk or save icon)
icon48.png: Larger version of the same icon
icon128.png: Even larger version
```

---

### How to Use:
1. Create a new folder with these files and the icons
2. In Chrome, go to `chrome://extensions/`
3. Enable "Developer mode" (toggle in top-right)
4. Click "Load unpacked" and select your folder
5. A new icon will appear in your toolbar
6. Visit any webpage, click the extension icon, and click "Save Page Content"

The page will be saved as an HTML file with a timestamp in your browser's default download location.

---

### Notes:
- The filename includes a timestamp to avoid overwrites
- Saves the full HTML content (including `<head>` and `<body>`)
- Works cross-origin due to "activeTab" permission
- Uses modern Manifest V3 API for compatibility with latest Chrome versions

